            <section id="why-selection-matters"></div>

<!-- WHY AEROSOL CAN MATERIAL SELECTION MATTERS -->
<section class="section" id="why-aerosol-can-material-selection-matters">
  <h2>Why Aerosol Can Material Selection Matters</h2>
  <p>Aerosol cans are pressure vessels that must safely contain liquefied or compressed propellants, maintain formulation stability for years, and remain compatible with automated high-speed filling lines. The choice of can material directly influences pressure tolerance, internal coating requirements, transport economics, and recyclability.</p>
                <p>An incorrect material choice can lead to corrosion failures, deformation, valve leakage, or regulatory non-compliance—issues that often surface only after products reach the market, when remediation is costly.</p>

                <h3>Key implications of can material selection include:</h3>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Factor</th>
                                <th>Implication</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Chemical Compatibility</strong></td>
                                <td>Long-term chemical compatibility with formulations and propellants</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Mechanical Integrity</strong></td>
                                <td>Mechanical integrity under pressure and temperature variation</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Manufacturing</strong></td>
                                <td>Filling line compatibility and crimp reliability</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Economics</strong></td>
                                <td>Total cost of ownership, not just per-can price</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="what-are-aluminium-cans">
</section>

<!-- WHAT ARE ALUMINIUM AEROSOL CANS? -->
<section class="section" id="what-are-aluminium-aerosol-cans">
  <h2>What Are Aluminium Aerosol Cans?</h2>
  <p>Aluminium aerosol cans are manufactured as seamless, single-piece containers using the impact extrusion process. A slug of aluminium is cold-formed into a cylindrical can body with an integral dome, eliminating side seams entirely. This seamless construction provides uniform wall thickness and excellent surface finish.</p>
                <p>Aluminium cans are widely used in cosmetics, pharmaceuticals, food aerosols, and premium personal care products, where aesthetics, weight reduction, and corrosion resistance are critical.</p>

                <h3>Key technical characteristics of aluminium aerosol cans:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Seamless one-piece construction with no welded joints</li>
                    <li>Naturally corrosion-resistant base metal</li>
                    <li>Lightweight structure reduces transport costs</li>
                    <li>Requires internal organic coatings for most formulations</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="what-are-tinplate-cans">
</section>

<!-- WHAT ARE TINPLATE AEROSOL CANS? -->
<section class="section" id="what-are-tinplate-aerosol-cans">
  <h2>What Are Tinplate Aerosol Cans?</h2>
  <p>Tinplate aerosol cans are made from tin-coated low-carbon steel sheets, formed into cylindrical bodies through deep drawing or three-piece welded construction. The base and dome are attached separately using mechanical seaming, resulting in a multi-component structure.</p>
                <p>Tinplate cans dominate automotive, industrial, household, paint, and insecticide aerosols, where mechanical strength, high-pressure tolerance, and cost efficiency are prioritized over aesthetics.</p>

                <figure>
                    
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/tinplate-aerosol-can-welded-seam-LUrNrVikIalvQyk0.png" 
          alt="Close-up of a tinplate aerosol can welded side seam" 
          width="1200" 
          height="800" 
          loading="lazy" 
        />
      </div>
      
                    <figcaption>Figure 2: The welded side seam characteristic of three-piece tinplate cans.</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <h3>Key technical characteristics of tinplate aerosol cans:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Multi-piece construction with welded side seam and seamed ends</li>
                    <li>Higher mechanical strength compared to aluminium</li>
                    <li>Heavier than aluminium, impacting logistics costs</li>
                    <li>Mandatory internal coatings to prevent corrosion</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="manufacturing-process">
</section>

<!-- MANUFACTURING PROCESS COMPARISON -->
<section class="section" id="manufacturing-process-comparison">
  <h2>Manufacturing Process Comparison</h2>
  <p>The manufacturing route defines not only the physical properties of the can but also its cost structure and quality control complexity. Aluminium and tinplate cans follow fundamentally different forming technologies, each with specific engineering trade-offs.</p>
                <p>Aluminium impact extrusion requires high-tonnage presses and precise slug metallurgy, while tinplate can production relies on sheet forming, welding, and multiple seaming operations.</p>

                <h3>Manufacturing Differences in Summary:</h3>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Aluminium Cans</th>
                                <th>Tinplate Cans</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Process Steps</strong></td>
                                <td>Impact extrusion, ironing, trimming, washing, coating, curing</td>
                                <td>Sheet cutting, body forming, welding, flanging, seaming</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Joints</strong></td>
                                <td>Fewer joints (seamless)</td>
                                <td>Multiple joints (weld + seams)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Leak Risk</strong></td>
                                <td>Lower leak-risk interfaces</td>
                                <td>Higher leak-risk due to seams</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Rigidity</strong></td>
                                <td>Ductile, lower rigidity</td>
                                <td>Higher dimensional rigidity</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="mechanical-strength">
</section>

<!-- MECHANICAL STRENGTH AND PRESSURE PERFORMANCE -->
<section class="section" id="mechanical-strength-and-pressure-performance">
  <h2>Mechanical Strength and Pressure Performance</h2>
  <p>Pressure resistance is a critical safety parameter governed by international aerosol standards. While both materials meet regulatory requirements when properly specified, their mechanical behavior differs under stress.</p>
                <p>Tinplate steel has higher yield strength and resists deformation more effectively at high internal pressures. Aluminium, while ductile, relies on precise wall thickness control and dome geometry for pressure resistance.</p>

                <figure>
                    
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/hydrostatic-burst-test-machine.jpg-ETfIt8tTERAR09th.png" 
          alt="Hydrostatic burst pressure testing machine for aerosol cans" 
          width="1200" 
          height="800" 
          loading="lazy" 
        />
      </div>
      
                    <figcaption>Figure 3: Burst pressure testing is essential to validate can strength.</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <h3>Performance characteristics:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Tinplate cans tolerate higher internal pressures with lower deformation</li>
                    <li>Aluminium cans deform plastically before rupture, offering visible warning signs</li>
                    <li>Both materials require compliance with pressure and burst testing standards</li>
                    <li>Final performance depends on thickness, dome design, and quality control</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="corrosion-behavior">
</section>

<!-- CORROSION BEHAVIOR AND INTERNAL COATINGS -->
<section class="section" id="corrosion-behavior-and-internal-coatings">
  <h2>Corrosion Behavior and Internal Coatings</h2>
  <p>Neither aluminium nor tinplate aerosol cans are universally corrosion-proof without coatings. Internal corrosion is influenced by formulation pH, water content, solvent polarity, and propellant chemistry.</p>
                <p>Aluminium forms a natural oxide layer that provides baseline corrosion resistance, but aggressive formulations still require epoxy, polyester, or BPA-NI coatings. Tinplate steel is inherently susceptible to corrosion and always requires a protective internal lacquer.</p>

                <figure>
                    
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/pitting-corrosion-aerosol-can-Yx6etsNY7rxHNQVz.jpg" 
          alt="Internal pitting corrosion inside an aerosol can" 
          width="1200" 
          height="800" 
          loading="lazy" 
        />
      </div>
      
                    <figcaption>Figure 4: Example of pitting corrosion failure inside an aerosol can.</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <h3>Key corrosion considerations:</h3>
                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Material</th>
                                <th>Coating Requirement</th>
                                <th>Risk Factor</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Tinplate</strong></td>
                                <td>Always requires internal coatings</td>
                                <td>High risk if coating fails</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Aluminium</strong></td>
                                <td>Required for most formulations</td>
                                <td>Water-based/acidic products pose risk</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </section>

