</section>

<!-- COST COMPARISON: ALUMINIUM VS TINPLATE AEROSOL CANS -->
<section class="section" id="cost-comparison-aluminium-vs-tinplate-aerosol-cans">
  <h2>Cost Comparison: Aluminium vs Tinplate Aerosol Cans</h2>
  <p>Cost comparison must consider total landed cost, not just ex-factory can pricing. Raw material prices, energy consumption, transport weight, and minimum order quantities all influence final economics.</p>
                <p>In general, tinplate cans are cheaper per unit at high volumes, while aluminium cans carry higher material and processing costs but offer logistics savings due to lower weight.</p>

                <figure>
                    
      <div class="image-slot">
        <img 
          src="https://assets.zyrosite.com/A3QlOV94WLSaL6xE/aluminium-vs-tinplate-aerosol-cost-comparison-4o8V6rxekZqaqRVa.png" 
          alt="Chart comparing cost drivers of aluminium vs tinplate cans" 
          width="1200" 
          height="800" 
          loading="lazy" 
        />
      </div>
      
                    <figcaption>Figure 5: Cost structure comparison between can materials.</figcaption>
                </figure>

                <h3>Cost drivers include:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Raw material pricing volatility (aluminium vs steel)</li>
                    <li>Energy intensity of impact extrusion</li>
                    <li>Transport and freight cost differences</li>
                    <li>MOQ and supplier availability by region</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="sustainability">
</section>

<!-- SUSTAINABILITY AND RECYCLING CONSIDERATIONS -->
<section class="section" id="sustainability-and-recycling-considerations">
  <h2>Sustainability and Recycling Considerations</h2>
  <p>Both aluminium and tinplate are fully recyclable and widely recovered through established recycling streams. Aluminium offers higher scrap value and requires significantly less energy to recycle compared to primary production.</p>
                <p>Tinplate recycling is also mature but involves separation processes to remove coatings and tin layers.</p>
                
                <h3>Sustainability comparison highlights:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Aluminium has higher recycling value and recovery rates</li>
                    <li>Recycled aluminium saves substantial energy vs virgin metal</li>
                    <li>Tinplate recycling is robust but less energy-efficient</li>
                    <li>Both materials support circular economy models</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

            <section id="selection-guide">
</section>

<!-- WHICH CAN IS BETTER FOR STARTUPS AND NEW BRANDS? -->
<section class="section" id="which-can-is-better-for-startups-and-new-brands">
  <h2>Which Can Is Better for Startups and New Brands?</h2>
  <p>There is no universally “better” material—only a better fit-for-purpose choice. Startups must balance aesthetics, cost, formulation compatibility, supply chain reliability, and scalability.</p>
                <p>Aluminium suits premium, low-volume, brand-driven products, while tinplate is often more practical for cost-sensitive, high-pressure, or industrial applications.</p>

                <div class="table-responsive">
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Goal / Requirement</th>
                                <th>Recommended Material</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Premium Aesthetics</strong></td>
                                <td>Aluminium (Seamless, high finish)</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Lightweight Logistics</strong></td>
                                <td>Aluminium</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>High Pressure / Industrial</strong></td>
                                <td>Tinplate</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td><strong>Cost Sensitive</strong></td>
                                <td>Tinplate</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>

                <h3>Selection guidance:</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Choose aluminium for premium positioning and lightweight logistics</li>
                    <li>Choose tinplate for high-pressure or cost-sensitive products</li>
                    <li>Validate compatibility through corrosion and stability testing</li>
                    <li>Consider supplier proximity and MOQs early</li>
                </ul>
            </section>

